Why Ephemeral Storage Fails

Containers have an ephemeral root filesystem. If a pod running a database crashes or gets rescheduled to another node, all writes stored inside the container disappear.

To solve this, Kubernetes decouples storage into three layers:

StorageClass (SC): The storage backend blueprint (e.g., AWS EBS, GCP PD, or local hostpath in Minikube) that enables dynamic provisioning.

PersistentVolume (PV): An actual piece of allocated storage in the cluster.

PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC): A request/ticket submitted by an application declaring how much storage and what access mode it needs.


+------------------------------------+
       |            StorageClass            | (Provisioner Engine)
       +-----------------+------------------+
                         |
                         v (Dynamically creates)
+---------------------------------------------------+
|               PersistentVolume (PV)               | (Cluster-wide resource)
|               Capacity: 1Gi, ReadWriteOnce        |
+------------------------+--------------------------+
                         |
                         | Bound to
                         v
+---------------------------------------------------+
|            PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)            | (Namespace-scoped request)
|            Requests: 1Gi                          |
+------------------------+--------------------------+
                         |
                         | Mounted as volume
                         v
+---------------------------------------------------+
|                     Pod Spec                      |
|            volumeMounts: /usr/share/nginx/html    |
+---------------------------------------------------+

Step 1: Check the Default StorageClass in Minikube

Minikube comes with a built-in dynamic provisioner out of the box:

kubectl get storageclass

You will see standard (default) using the k8s.io/minikube-hostpath provisioner.

Step 2: Create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)

Save this manifest as storage-demo.yaml:

cat <<EOF > storage-demo.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: web-data-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 500Mi
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: persistent-web
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: persistent-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: persistent-web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        volumeMounts:
        - name: html-storage
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
      volumes:
      - name: html-storage
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: web-data-pvc
EOF

kubectl apply -f storage-demo.yaml


Step 3: Verify Dynamic Binding

Check that Minikube automatically created a backing PV and bound it to your claim:

kubectl get pvc web-data-pvc
kubectl get pv

Both should show a status of Bound.

Step 4: Test Data Persistence Across Pod Deletion

Write a custom file onto the mounted volume inside the pod:

POD_NAME=$(kubectl get pods -l app=persistent-web -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")

# Write a persistent message
kubectl exec $POD_NAME -- sh -c 'echo "Kubernetes Day 2 Storage Confirmed" > /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html'

# Verify the file is served
kubectl exec $POD_NAME -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

The terminal should output: Kubernetes Day 2 Storage Confirmed.

Step 2: Delete the Pod to Test Persistence

Delete the current running pod:

Now, kill the pod and watch Kubernetes spawn a replacement:

kubectl delete pod $POD_NAME

# Wait for the replacement pod to be Running
kubectl get pods -l app=persistent-web -w

Once the new pod is running, verify that the data survived:

Step 3: Confirm Data Survived the Deletion

Retrieve the new pod's name and read the file from the persistent volume:

NEW_POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app=persistent-web -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")
kubectl exec $NEW_POD -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

Run these commands and paste the final cat output.

Kubernetes Day 2 Storage Confirmed

Verification: The terminal should still output Kubernetes Day 2 Storage Confirmed, proving that the data was safely stored on the underlying PersistentVolume and re-mounted to the new pod.


